{"title":"Breaking one factor into a product","key_idea":"You can split one factor into two smaller factors and keep the multiplication result the same.","method":["Look at the factor you need to break apart.","Find two smaller factors that multiply to make that number.","Write the multiplication with all three factors.","Check: multiply the two smaller factors; the result should equal the original factor."],"worked_example":{"problem":"7 x 10 = 7 x 5 x __","steps":["The factor 10 needs to be split into two smaller factors.","Find two numbers that multiply to make 10: 5 and 2.","Write: 7 x 10 = 7 x 5 x 2.","Check: 5 x 2 = 10, which is correct."],"answer":"2"},"key_terms":[{"term":"factor","meaning":"a number you multiply with another number"},{"term":"split or break apart","meaning":"divide a number into smaller numbers that multiply back to the original"}],"common_mistakes":["Forgetting to check that the two smaller factors multiply back to the original factor.","Writing factors that do not multiply correctly to give the original number."],"remember":"When you split a factor, the two new factors must multiply to give that original factor.","quick_check":[{"q":"In 5 x 14 = 5 x 7 x __, what number goes in the blank?","a":"2"},{"q":"In 4 x 15 = 4 x 3 x __, what number goes in the blank?","a":"5"}],"tell_your_child":"When you see a number that can be split into smaller parts, you can write it as a multiplication of those parts. Just make sure the two smaller numbers multiply back to give you the original number."}
